## 1. Introduction
Electromagnetic wave (EMW) absorption materials with high absorption capacity and wide absorption bandwidth are beneficial to weaken the harm of electronic equipment to human health. Furthermore, the preparation of lightweight absorbers is still urgent due to the actual requirements of military defense [1,2]. Therefore, the mitigation of EMW pollution has aroused the interest of international researchers. Generally speaking, absorbers will realize energy conversion through their own loss mechanisms, thus achieving the purpose of attenuating EMW [3-5]. Considering the synergistic effect of loss mechanism on the absorption performance of EMW, researchers usually use a variety of loss mechanisms to enhance the attenuation of EMW, including magnetic loss, dielectric loss and reflections [6,7]. To achieve the coexistence of the above loss mechanisms, it can be achieved to optimize the composition and structure of the absorbers.
In terms of components, researchers have prepared a large number of single-component EMW materials that can provide single loss mechanisms, such as carbon, conductive polymers, magnetic metals and magnetic metal alloy [8-10]. Furthermore, dielectric materials have the advantages of good stability and a large window for the tuning of permittivity. Theoretically, the construction of multi-component EMW materials should satisfy the advantages of low density, good stability, and uniform distribution among the components and provide a variety of loss mechanisms to attenuate EMW [11,12]. Therefore, many researchers are committed to prepare porous composite materials with multiple loss mechanisms, resulting in the introduction of more transmission paths and synergistic loss effects to promote the dissipation of EMW [13-15]. Recently, manganese oxides have been widely studied in the fields of EMW absorption owing to its low cost and abundant resources. For examples, BDC/MnO nanorods composites [16], MnOnrs/PBDC composites [17], ${\mathrm{Fe/MnO@C}}$ composites [18] and Co/MnO composites [19] displayed excellent EMW absorption performance because of the extension of the EMW path and polarization loss. Nonetheless, the complicated synthetic approaches and uncontrollable microstructure are the important reasons to limit the practical application of EMW absorption materials.
In addition to adjusting the composition of the absorbers, the preparation of the special structure is also conducive to enhancing the EMW loss ability of the materials [20,21]. As a kind of crystal material, the instability of microstructure of metal organic frameworks (MOFs) materials makes many researchers try to adjust the preparation methods to obtain different structures [22,23]. After calcination, the obtained composites can still inherit the microstructure of the precursors, thus regulating the microstructure of the absorbers. Ouyang and co-author [24] prepared uniform flower spheroidal precursors by hydrothermal method and calcined into hollow derivatives. Taking advantage of the orientation growth characteristics of precursors, Jang and co-author [25] successfully synthesized the precursor with different microstructures by changing the type of solvents. In addition, the structure of the precursors can be changed by adjusting the composition of the MOFs. Owing to the different coordination ability between metal ions and ligands, Wang and co-author [26] prepared Ni-Co-MOF precursors with different microstructures by adjusting the proportion of metal ions, and the corresponding derivatives with similar microstructures were obtained after calcination. Correspondingly, Zhang and co-author [27] changed the structure of MOF precursors by adding a variety of ligands. Amazingly, MOFs can be easily etched due to its structural instability, thus forming a unique microstructure. Pan and co-author [28] prepared core–shell composites by selectively etching ZIF-8 precursors. Cui and co-author [29] synthesized hollow microstructure by alkaline etching. Therefore, etching method becomes a hot research direction for adjusting the microstructure of MOFs precursors.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Reagents
Cobalt tetrahydrate acetate $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H_{2}O})$ , 2,5-dihydroxy-terephthalic acid (DHTA), Manganese chloride $\begin{array}{r}{(\ensuremath{\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}})}\end{array}$ , ammonium chloride $(N H_{4}C l)$ and ammonia solution $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}0\right)$ were obtained from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd.
## 2.2. Preparation of Co-MOF-74 nanorods
Co-MOF-74 nanorods were synthesized by a facile method based on the literature method [30]. Briefly, the transparent solution A was obtained by dissolving 4 mmol ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{CH}}_{3}{\mathrm{COO}})_{2}{\cdot}4{\mathrm{H}}_{2}0$ in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water, and the transparent solution B was produced by dissolving 2 mmol 2,5-dihydroxy-terephthalicacid (DHTA) in another $50~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water. Then, solution A and B were placed into round-bottomed flask in an oil bath. After stirring for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ , the mixture solution was reacted at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for one hour. The precipitates were washed with deionized water and ethanol, and freeze-drying.
## 2.3. Preparation of CoMn-MOF-74 nanorods
CoMn-MOF-74 nanorods with rough surface was synthesized by etching method [28]. In a typical synthesis, $_{0.2\mathrm{~g~}}$ of the asprepared Co-MOF-74 nanorods was dispersed in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water by ultrasonic dispersion. Then $0.8~\mathrm{mL}$ of ${\sf N H}_{3}{\cdot}{\sf H}_{2}0$ and 6 mmol $\mathsf{N H}_{4}\mathsf{C l}$ were dispersed in another $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of ultrapure water by ultrasonic dispersion. Subsequently, $0.2\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{MnCl_{2}}$ was added into the mixture under stirring. Finally, the mixture was added into the Co-MOF-74 nanorods solution and stirred for X h $\mathrm{\Delta}X=3$ , 9, 15). The samples were collected by centrifugation, washed with water and ethanol, and freeze-dried. The obtained samples were named as CoMn-MOF-74-3, CoMn-MOF-74-9 and CoMn-MOF-74-15.
## 2.4. Preparation of hierarchical Co@C@MnO nanorods
The as-synthesized CoMn-MOF-74 nanorods were decomposed at $700^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ under argon in a tubular furnace. The final samples were labeled as Co@C@MnO-3, Co@C@MnO-9 and Co@C@MnO-15, respectively.
## 2.5. Characterization
The phase and crystal structure of the samples were characterized by X-ray diffraction (XRD, Bruker, D8 Advance, Germany). The scanning electron microscopy with field-emission (FE-SEM, Hitachi, S-4800, Japan) and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (TEM, FEI Tecnai G2 F20 S-TWIN, America) were adopted to investigate the morphologies and structures of the samples. Raman spectroscopy was analyzed by a Raman spectrometer (Renishaw Invia Raman spectrometer, Britain). $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorptiondesorption isotherms were obtained by the Brunauer-EmmettTeller (BET, ASAP2460, Micrometrics, America). The coaxial line method was used to measure the EM parameters through a vector network (Agilent, HP8753D, America) analyzer in the frequency region of $2{\cdot}18\ \mathsf{G H z}$ . The tested samples were prepared by mixing the products with paraffin wax at a mass ratio of 20:80 and then pressed into toroidal-shaped samples (inner diameter $=3.04~\mathrm{{mm}}$ ; outer diameter $\mathbf{\omega}=7~\mathrm{mm}$ ).
## 3. Results and discussion
The synthesis process of hierarchical $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods is illustrated in scheme 1. In this process, we use the method of optimizing published literature. Firstly, the Co-MOF-74 precursors were decomposed by the etching of ammonia, and then some ligands are released. When the $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}$ were coordinated with the ligands, the CoMn-MOF-74 nanorods with rough surface were formed. Finally, the CoMn-MOF-74 nanorods were decomposed at $700^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to obtain hierarchical $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods.
The morphology of the CoMn-MOF-74 nanorods are observed by SEM, as shown in Fig. 1(a-c). Obviously, the results show that the morphologies of all the precursors show the same characteristics of nanorods. Besides, the surface roughness of nanorods increases significantly with the increase of etching time. To characterize the composition of the etched sample, the XRD diffraction pattern is displayed in Fig. 1(d). Compared with the simulated diffraction peaks of MOF-74, it can be found that the prepared precursors are CoMn-MOF-74 with good crystallinity and without any impurity phase, proving that $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}$ really participate in the preparation of CoMn-MOF-74 precursors.
The SEM images in Fig. 2 are to verify that the calcined $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ composites can still maintain the original morphology. As displayed in Fig. 2(a1-c1), all samples were nanorods with rough surface, which further indicate that the calcination process will not have a great influence on the framework of nanorods. It can be seen from SEM images with larger magnification that the roughness of the calcined $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods surface increases with the extension of etching time, which further shows that etching time makes a big difference to the morphology of the composites (Fig. 2(a2-c2)).
The TEM images of the $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods are presented in Fig. 3(a-c), which obviously shows that the framework of carbon nanorods contains a large number of uniformly distributed particles. Compared with the $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–3}$ and $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–9}$ nanorods, the ${\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–15}}$ nanorods significantly have fewer Co nanoparticles and more MnO nanoparticles in the carbon framework of ${\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–15}}$ nanorods, which further proved that $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ can replace a small amount of $C0^{2+}$ and then coordinated with the ligand. The elemental mapping results are showed in Fig. 3(d-i), which are verified the presence of the C, Co, Mn and O elemental in the carbon framework, indicating that Co and MnO nanoparticles exist in the carbon framework.
Through a series of advanced characterization, the structural information of the hierarchical $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods are determined. As presented in Fig. 4(a), it can be seen that all the products have (111), (200), and (220) planes, which respectively correspond to the diffraction peaks of metallic Co (JCPDS No. 15- 0806), demonstrating that the $C0^{2+}$ are reduced to metallic Co during the calcination process [31]. In addition to the diffraction peaks described above, all the products have several distinct diffraction peaks, which stands for the (111), (200), (220) (311) and (222) planes of MnO (JCPDS No. 07-0230) [32]. Besides, compared with the $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–3}$ and $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–9}$ nanorods, the diffraction peaks of ${\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–15}}$ nanorods become sharpest and intensest, illustrating the best crystallinity of the ${\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–15}}$ nanorods. The graphitized crystal structure of $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods were further characterized by Raman spectroscopy. In general, there are two main characteristic peaks in carbon structure, which are disordered carbon (D band) at around $1340^{-1}$ and graphite carbon (G band) at around $1580~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [11]. Moreover, the graphitization degree of the products can be evaluated by the intensity ratio of D band and G band $\left(\mathrm{I_{D}}/\mathrm{I_{G}}\right)$ . As illustrated in Fig. 4(b), the $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ intensity values of $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–3}$ , Co@C@MnO-9 and Co@C@MnO-15 nanorods are 0.99, 0.96, and 0.87, respectively. Obviously, the gradually decreasing $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ intensity values means a higher degree of graphitization, which will help to improve the conduction loss that causes the enhanced EMW absorption performance of the products [33].
Due to the important research value of lightweight absorbers in the field of EMW absorption, researchers usually prepare porous materials to meet this characteristic. Generally, according to Maxwell-Garnett theory, porous structures can reduce the composite permittivity and balance impedance matching. The porous structure can provide a rich interface and promote the interface polarization. In addition, the porous structure is also conducive to the preparation of lightweight EMW absorbers [34-36]. The specific surface area and pore size distribution of the $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods were investigated by ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption measurements. As presented in Fig. 5(a), the $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods show a type-III adsorption-desorption isotherm curve and the specific surface area of $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–3}$ , $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–9}$ and ${\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–15}}$ nanorods estimated by Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method, was 118.7, 123.9, and $\mathrm{~i~}31.9\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. This result indicates that the calcined products with more etching time have a larger specific surface area, thus providing more interfaces to cause interface polarization loss. The mesoporous structures in all of the $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods are mainly revealed by the pore size distribution diagrams (Fig. 5(b)).
Based on the Marksworth’s EM field theory, the EMW absorption capacity of the absorbers is determined by two parameters: the relative complex permittivity $\left(\mathbb{E}_{\Gamma}=\mathbb{E}^{\prime}{-}\mathrm{j}\mathbb{E}^{\prime\prime}\right)$ and the relative complex permeability $\left(\upmu_{\mathrm{r}}=\upmu^{\prime}{-}\mathrm{j}\upmu^{\prime\prime}\right)$ , which have a decisive effect on the reflectivity and attenuation characteristics of the absorbers respectively [37-39]. As exhibited in Fig. 6 (a), the $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ values of the all $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods show a downward trend with increasing frequency, which can be ascribe to the polarization processes. Besides, the ${\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–15}}$ nanorods show the highest $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ values over the entire test frequency range, which is due to the increase of the interface between carbon, MnO and Co in the product, resulting in enhancing the interfacial polarization loss induced by the increased electron accumulation at the interface [10,40]. Moreover, the $\bar{\mathbf{\varepsilon}}^{\prime\prime}$ values of the $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–15}$ nanorods are higher than the $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O-3}$ and $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–9}$ nanorods, meaning that the increased interface caused by more etching time and the introduction of more MnO can effectively improve the EMW loss capacity of the absorbers. The fluctuations in the permittivity curve may be derived from multiple reflections and multiple polarizations (Fig. 6(b)). To investigate the dielectric loss capacity of $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods, the dielectric loss tangent $(\mathrm{tan}\updelta_{\mathrm{\varepsilon}}=\upvarepsilon^{\prime\prime}/\upvarepsilon^{\prime})$ are obtained and exhibited in Fig. 6 (c). Obviously, the $\tan\delta_{\mathrm{{z}}}$ values of the ${\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–15}}$ and $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O-9}$ nanorods are higher than the $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–3}$ nanorods, demonstrating that more EMW energy is dissipated. Due to the existence of magnetic Co nanoparticles, the magnetic loss is another important factor to improve the EMW absorption properties. As displayed in Fig. 6(d), for the $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–3}$ , ${\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–15}}$ and $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–9}$ nanorods, the $\upmu^{\prime}$ values show a decreasing trend. All the $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods exhibit a little different $\upmu^{\prime\prime}$ values, indicating a similar magnetic dissipation capability (Fig. 6(e)). All of the $\upmu^{\prime}$ values fluctuate in $2-$ $18~\mathrm{GHz}$ (Fig. $6(\mathrm{d})_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ ), and the corresponding curve of $\upmu^{\prime\prime}$ values also show obvious fluctuations in $2{\cdot}18\ \mathrm{GHz}$ (Fig. 6(e)), indicating that the existence of the natural ferromagnetic resonance, exchange resonance and multiple reflections. [26]. To discuss the magnetic loss ability of different $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods, the magnetic loss tangent $(\mathrm{{tan}\mathrm{{\delta_{\mu}=}\mu^{\prime\prime}/\mu^{\prime}})}$ are obtained and showed in Fig. 6 (f). It is can be found that the $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods possess the similar ability to dissipate EMW energy through magnetic loss. Furthermore, the $\tan\delta_{\mathrm{{g}}}$ values of all the $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods exceed 0.3, which are larger than their $\tan\updelta_{\upmu}$ values. Thus, a conclusion can be drawn that the dielectric loss is the main factor causing EMW absorption of $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods.
Generally, the dielectric loss induced by polarization and conduction loss leads to the dissipation of EMW. The main mechanism of polarization loss is dipole and interfacial polarization in microwave range. Generally, dipole polarization is derived from molecules with dipole moments. While the interfacial polarization usually exists in a heterogeneous structure [41]. The relationship between $\mathbf{\delta}\mathbf{\varepsilon}^{\prime}$ and $\bar{\varepsilon}^{\prime\prime}$ is used to investigate the dielectric loss mechanisms of the products. Thus, From Debye’s theory, it can be concluded that the complex permittivity satisfies the following formula [42,43]:
$$
\left(\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}^{\prime}-\frac{\varepsilon_{s}+\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}+\left(\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}^{\prime\prime}\right)^{2}=\left(\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}
$$
Theoretically, it can be found that a single semicircle in the plot of $\mathbf{\delta}\mathbf{\varepsilon}^{\prime}$ and $\bar{\mathbf{\varepsilon}}^{\prime\prime}$ is called as a Cole-Cole semicircle, which stands for one Debye relaxation process. Clearly, more than one distorted semicircles are existed in the curves of all $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods, which were caused by the presence of a multiple polarization relaxation process (Fig. 7 (a-c)). Clearly, it can be inferred that the relaxation process comes from the interface polarization caused by the heterogeneous interfaces between Co, MnO and C as well as the dipole polarization induced by the defects and oxygen functional groups in the carbon matrix. Besides, according to Fig. 5, it can be concluded that ${\mathsf{C o@C@M n O–15}}$ nanorods has the largest specific surface area, which can provide abundant interface, leading to the enhancement of the interface polarization loss. Furthermore, the existence of the straight-line part in the $\mathbf{\delta}\mathbf{\varepsilon}^{\prime}$ and $\bar{\mathbf{\varepsilon}}^{\prime\prime}$ curves can be ascribed to the conduction loss induced by the graphite carbon [44].
To analyse the mechanism of magnetic loss, the plots of $\upmu^{\prime\prime}(\upmu^{\prime})^{-2}f^{-1}$ for $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods are shown in Fig. 7 (d) and calculated by the following formula [45]. It is observed that the values of $\upmu^{\prime\prime}(\upmu^{\prime})^{-2}f^{-1}$ varies greatly in the frequency range of $2{\mathrm{-}}18{\mathrm{~GHz}},$ indicating that the eddy current loss has little effect on the EMW absorption. Therefore, the magnetic loss mechanisms mainly come from natural resonance and exchange resonance.
$$
C_{0}=\mu^{\prime\prime}(\mu^{\prime})^{-2}f^{-1}
$$
To characterize the EMW absorption performance of the absorbers more intuitively, RL values are calculated by the following formulas according to the transmission line theory: [46,47]:
$$
R_{L}(d B)=20\mathrm{log}\Bigg|\frac{Z_{i n}-Z_{0}}{Z_{i n}+Z_{0}}\Bigg|
$$
$$
Z_{i n}=Z_{0}\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{r}}{\varepsilon_{r}}}\mathrm{tan}h\left[j\frac{2\pi}{c}\sqrt{\mu_{r}\varepsilon_{r}}f d\right]
$$
The attenuation constant needs to be considered in EMW absorption properties. Theoretically, the attenuation constant ensures that EMW entering the material are attenuated to a large extent, which can be calculated by the following formula [48]:
$$
\alpha=\frac{\sqrt{2}\pi f}{c}\times\sqrt{\left(\mu^{\prime\prime}\pmb{\varepsilon}^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\pmb{\varepsilon}^{\prime}\right)+\sqrt{\left(\mu^{\prime\prime}\pmb{\varepsilon}^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\pmb{\varepsilon}^{\prime}\right)^{2}+\left(\mu^{\prime}\pmb{\varepsilon}^{\prime\prime}+\mu^{\prime\prime}\pmb{\varepsilon}^{\prime}\right)^{2}}}
$$
As displayed in Fig. 9, It is can be found that the $\textsf{\textsf{a}}$ values of the $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods increase gradually, which is mainly caused by enhanced interfacial polarization loss. Additionally, the rich dipoles of the Co@C@MnO nanorods are conducive to promote the dipole polarization loss.
Fig. 10 describes the EM wave absorption mechanisms of the $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods. Firstly, the microstructure of $\mathsf{C o@C@M n O}$ nanorods can form abundant heterogeneous interface to enhance interfacial polarization loss. The graphite carbon provides electronic transportation paths, resulting in the enhancement of the conduction loss. The dipole polarization loss induced from porous carbon with a large number of dipoles can further enhance the dielectric loss. Due to the hierarchical porous structures, the multiple reflections and scatterings are conducive to the dissipation of EMW. Secondly, the enhanced magnetic loss attributed to the exchange resonance and natural resonance can promote the improvement of the impedance matching. As a result, the complex composition and unique heterostructure can ensure that the absorbers show the excellent EM wave absorption performance.
